{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices (typically +1 to +6 EUR, increasing with horizon). Apply a downward level shift to the entire path; larger shift for longer horizons.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift error: the model underestimates the strength of sustained downtrends (realized keeps falling while forecast flattens/recovers). Add extra negative slope when the last 2–4 weeks show a clear decline (momentum/trend reinforcement).",
    "Mean-reversion overshoot: the model reverts upward too quickly after a drop (predicts rebound/plateau), but realized continues to grind lower. Reduce mean-reversion strength; delay/attenuate rebounds unless there is confirmed reversal in recent history.",
    "Late-horizon overprediction: errors widen notably after ~day 10–15, where forecasts stay ~70–76 while realized moves into low/mid-60s. Use a progressive downward adjustment that grows with lead time (e.g., linear ramp).",
    "Turning-point lag: when the market is trending down, the forecast turns up prematurely (false bottom). Require stronger evidence for a trend change (e.g., multiple higher closes) before allowing forecast inflection."
  ]
}
